        <div id="how-dish-soap-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Dish Soap Actually Does
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Surfactants Lift Grease</h3>

          <p>
            Hand dishwashing liquid is a blend of surfactants, water and a few helpers. A surfactant molecule has one end that clings to water and one end that clings to oil, so it surrounds grease, lifts it off the plate and holds it in the rinse water so it does not settle back on the next glass. Almost every bottle on the shelf leans on the same small family of surfactants: sodium lauryl sulfate, sodium laureth sulfate, amine oxides and betaines. The brand matters less than how much of that blend is in the bottle.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What a Typical Formula Contains</h3>

          <p>
            Dawn Ultra's ingredient list shows the pattern clearly. It pairs sodium lauryl sulfate and sodium laureth sulfate with C10-16 alkyldimethylamine oxide, which the maker describes as a cleaning booster. Palmolive Ultra Pure + Clear uses sodium laureth sulfate with lauramidopropyl betaine. Dr. Bronner's Sal Suds uses sodium lauryl sulfate with coco-betaine and lauryl glucoside. Different marketing, overlapping chemistry. What separates them is concentration, fragrance, dyes and preservatives, and whether an outside body has reviewed the formula.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Narrow Product by Design</h3>

          <p>
            Dish soap is also a narrow product. It is designed to foam heavily, rinse clean and be gentle enough for bare hands in a sink of warm water. Those same traits make it the wrong choice for an automatic dishwasher, a pressure washer tank or a floor machine, which we cover further down. Buying well means matching the bottle to the sink, the grease load and the skin that will be in the water, not picking the loudest label.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sources We Used</h3>

          <p>
            We did not run a lab test. We built this list from ingredient disclosures, manufacturers' product pages and SmartLabel listings, the EPA Safer Choice program's public criteria, retailer label images and long-running owner reports. For each product we checked what surfactants it lists first, whether it is sold as a concentrate, whether a fragrance-free version exists, which certifications appear on the bottle itself, and what the label says about dishwashers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How We Reported Certifications</h3>

          <p>
            Certifications are reported only where we could tie them to the product. Seventh Generation states that its dish liquids carry EPA Safer Choice certification, and its Free and Clear bottle also carries the USDA Certified Biobased label. For other brands we describe what the maker claims, such as plant-derived ingredients or cruelty-free status, without upgrading those claims into certifications. If a green logo matters to you, look for it on the exact bottle and scent, because listings change by variant.
          </p>

