        <div id="concentration" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Concentration: Why Some Bottles Go Further
          </h2>

          <p>
            The word Ultra on a dish soap bottle has a history. Decades ago the big brands concentrated their formulas so a smaller bottle did the work of a larger one, and the label stuck. Today Ultra mostly signals a concentrated formula, but the term is not regulated, and two Ultra bottles from different brands can perform very differently. The most reliable test is your own sink: if a teaspoon handles a full load of greasy dishes, the soap is concentrated enough.
          </p>

          <p>
            Concentration matters more than price per bottle. A watery store-brand soap that needs three squirts per sinkful costs more per washed dish than a concentrated name brand that needs one. Owners commonly report that Dawn Ultra and Palmolive Ultra go noticeably further than budget house brands, and that Sal Suds, which is sold as an all-purpose concentrate, goes furthest of all when diluted the way the maker suggests.
          </p>

