        <div id="grease" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Grease, Baked-On Food and the Spray Question
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where the Differences Show Up</h3>

          <p>
            For ordinary plates and glasses almost any decent dish soap works. The differences show up on roasting pans, sheet pans and skillets with cooked-on fat, where surfactant strength and contact time decide whether you scrub for thirty seconds or five minutes. Here Dawn's reputation is earned: its marketing leans on the fact that wildlife rescue groups use Dawn to clean birds caught in oil spills, which says something about how well the formula lifts oil without being harsh on skin or feathers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Reading the Grease Claims on the Bottle</h3>

          <p>
            Dawn's Platinum line is pitched as a step above Ultra for grease. Be careful with the numbers on the bottle: the maker's five-times-faster claims are measured against non-Ultra Dawn, not against Ultra itself, so the real gap between Ultra and Platinum is smaller than the label suggests. Platinum is worth it if you cook with a lot of fat; for most households Ultra is enough.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How the Powerwash Spray Works</h3>

          <p>
            The Powerwash spray changes the method rather than the chemistry. You spray it directly on a dry or lightly wet greasy dish, wait briefly on heavily soiled items, then wipe and rinse. The maker describes it as working on contact without water. Owners commonly report it is the best format for a single greasy pan or a few dishes between full washes, because you do not fill a sink. The trade-off is cost per use, and the refill bottles are the way to keep that reasonable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sensitive-skin" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Sensitive Skin and Fragrance-Free Formulas
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Soap Is Rarely the Only Cause</h3>

          <p>
            If washing dishes leaves your hands red, dry or itchy, the soap may be one cause, but rarely the only one. Hot water and long soaks strip natural oils from skin regardless of the formula, and surfactants make that worse. The American Academy of Dermatology recommends wearing gloves for wet work like dishwashing if you have hand dermatitis, and that single change usually helps more than switching brands.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fragrance and Preservatives</h3>

          <p>
            When the soap itself is the problem, fragrance and preservatives are the usual suspects. Fragrance is one of the most common causes of allergic contact dermatitis, and some preservatives used in household cleaners, including methylisothiazolinone, are recognised contact allergens. Methylisothiazolinone appears in the Dawn Ultra ingredient list we reviewed. That does not make Dawn unsafe for most people, but it is a reason for anyone with known sensitivities to read labels.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Our Pick for Sensitive Skin</h3>

          <p>
            For sensitive skin we point first to Palmolive Ultra Pure + Clear in its unscented version, which Colgate-Palmolive lists as hypoallergenic with no dyes, parabens or phosphates and a short ingredient list. Seventh Generation Free and Clear is the second choice, with no added fragrance or dyes and an EPA Safer Choice review behind it. Ecover's Zero dish soap is another fragrance-free option where you can find it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="eco-labels" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Eco Certifications: What the Logos Mean
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">EPA Safer Choice</h3>

          <p>
            The most useful mark on a US dish soap is EPA Safer Choice. The EPA's program reviews every intentionally added ingredient against health and environmental criteria, including aquatic toxicity and biodegradability, and requires full ingredient disclosure. There is also a Safer Choice Fragrance-Free mark for products with no fragrance at all. Seventh Generation's dish liquids carry Safer Choice, which is a large part of why it is our eco pick. You can check any product in the EPA's searchable Safer Choice database.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Narrower Marks</h3>

          <p>
            Other marks answer narrower questions. USDA Certified Biobased tells you what share of the product's carbon comes from plants rather than petroleum, which is useful but says nothing about toxicity. Leaping Bunny certifies that no animal testing happened at any stage, including by suppliers, which is an ethical claim rather than an environmental one. Ecover holds Leaping Bunny certification for its range, and Method and Mrs. Meyer's market their products as not tested on animals.
          </p>

